, I can form a Chi of T1-T2-T3. \n\nSo, in that case, keeping T2 is important. \n\nTherefore, the best option is to discard a tile that doesn't interfere with forming the Chi. \n\nSo between B2, B5, W5, W3. \n\nIf I discard B2, I can keep B5. B5 is a middle tile. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W3, since there are two left, maybe safe. \n\nBut I already have a W3 in my hand and in the Chi. \n\nHmm. \n\nAnother thought: My hand has a Pung of F1 (East Wind), which is both the prevalent and seat wind. That's worth 2 points each. So that's 4 points. The Chi of W3-W4-W5 is an All Chows pattern (2 points). If I can form another Chi and a pair, that's more points. \n\nIf I form a Chi of T1-T2-T3 and a pair, that's two Chows. All Chows is 2 points. Plus the Pung of F1. \n\nSo the total points would be 4 (from F1) + 2 (All Chows) + others. \n\nTo form that Chi, I need to keep T2. Therefore, I must not discard T2. \n\nSo among the remaining options, the best is to discard B2 or B5. \n\nBetween those, B2 is part of a possible Chi with B1 and B3. But since B1 and B3 are still in the unshown tiles (1 each), if I discard B2, someone could take it. \n\nBut if I discard B5, it's part of a Chi with B4 and B6. B4 has 4 left, B6 has 1. \n\nSo B2 is more likely to be needed. \n\nTherefore, better to discard B5. \n\nSo the answer would be Play B5.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discarding B5 allows retaining T2 for potential T1-T2-T3 Chi, while B5 is less critical for others' melds compared to B2.  \nReason: Keeping T2 is crucial for forming a T1-T2-T3 Chi, which complements existing melds. B5 is safer to discard as it\u2019s less likely to complete others\u2019 Chows.  \nAnswer: Play B5"}
