meone discards a W4, I can form a Chi of [W3, W4, W5]. But I already have that Chi in my melds. So maybe this is a duplicate? That doesn't make sense. Perhaps the current action is not about W4 but another tile. Wait, the valid actions are \"Chi W4; Pass\". So the system is suggesting that I can perform a Chi on W4. But given that I already have a Chi for W4, why is this possible?\n\nWait, maybe the current situation is that the previous player discarded a W4, and I can form a Chi. But I already have a Chi for W4. How can I form another one? Unless there's a mistake here. Alternatively, maybe the notation is that the Chi W4 refers to a different sequence. Let me think again. The existing Chi is [W3, W4, W5]. If I have W3 and W5 in my hand, and someone discards a W4, I can form that Chi. But if I already have that Chi, then another W4 discard might not be useful. Unless I have another set. Wait, my hand tiles are W3 and W5. So if I have those two, and someone discards a W4, I can form a Chi. But I already did that. So perhaps the current action is a mistake, or there's a misunderstanding. Alternatively, maybe the current action is to form a different Chi. For example, if I have T1 and T2, and someone discards T3, I can form a Chi of T1-T2-T3. But in this case, the Chi W4 is already formed. \n\nWait, maybe the current valid action is not about forming a new Chi but about a different sequence. Let me check the unshown tiles. The unshown tiles include W4: 0. Wait, the unshown tiles for W4 are 0, meaning all W4s have been revealed. So if someone is discarding a W4, that's possible. But how can I form another Chi? Because I already have a Chi of W3-W4-W5. Unless there's a mistake in the melds. Let me check the melds again. The melds are [[F1, F1, F1], [W3, W4, W5]]. So the second meld is a Chi of W3, W4, W5. Therefore, if another W4 is discarded, I can't form another Chi because I don't have the necessary tiles. Wait, unless I have another W3 and W5 in my hand. But accord