ve higher chances to form pairs or melds.\n\nBetween the options, T9 is a terminal and has only one left. Discarding T9 might be safe. Alternatively, W6 is a middle tile but no possible melds. Let's compare.\n\nIf I discard T9, I remove a terminal tile. If I discard W6, I remove a middle tile. Since I don't have any melds that include W6, maybe it's better to discard W6. But why?\n\nWait, looking at the unshown tiles, W6 has 4 left. So if I discard W6, there are still four in the wall. But if I discard T9, which has only one left, maybe someone else is holding it. But since the unshown count is 1, it's likely that someone else has it or it's in the wall.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard a tile that others might need. For example, looking at the game history of other players, if they have melds that include certain tiles, I should avoid discarding those.\n\nThe previous player has a meld of W7, W8, W9. So they might not need W6. The player across has a meld of T7, T8, T9. So they might not need T9. The next player hasn't formed any melds except some plays. So maybe T9 is safe to discard.\n\nSo between T9 and W6, I think T9 is safer to discard. Also, keeping W6 might be useful if I can form a meld later. But since I can't form any meld with W6, it's not helpful. So maybe discard T9.\n\nAnother angle: my hand has T9 as a single tile. If I discard it, it might help someone else complete a meld. But since the player across has T7, T8, T9, they already have that. So they don't need another T9. The next player has played T9 before. So maybe they already have it. So discarding T9 is safe.\n\nTherefore, the best action is to Play T9.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discard T9 as it is a terminal tile with low utility for forming additional melds and is less likely to be needed by others given existing melds.]\nReason: [T9 is a terminal tile with no potential for forming chows or pungs in the current hand. The player across already has a meld of T7, T8, T9, making T9 less valuable 